  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "What is the distance between the points $(3,2)$  and $(3,-4)?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/76b886c17d4bd918a2de824ee9afd736eacf6f9fbb178729e54b4f738364b72f.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/f9ea01d7d94fe81c417f1df698da2475f622c24338020d84f1cfd8bf1ec86364.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/dcdfbbe30584614af5eb9e40783b2c3016f85be13b4653e1455a3430c16a609c.png)\n\nThe point $(3,2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(3,-4)$ is at a distance of $4$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $4+2=6.$",
